{"type":"text","timestamp":1789825299591,"sessionID":"ses_f462a6b74ffedf3OBDp51fgDmj","part":{"id":"prt_0b9e69b9c0011g56fSO03mxsYF_text-0","sessionID":"ses_f462a6b74ffedf3OBDp51fgDmj","messageID":"msg_0b9e69b9c0011g56fSO03mxsYF","type":"text","text":"The workbook is complete and verified. Here is the delivery.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n**`/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/petsmart.xlsx`** — four visible sheets (`Deal ledger`, `Summary`, `Questions`, `Source text`) plus the hidden `Lists` sheet; 45 ledger rows, 14 visible columns with the remaining fields in collapsed, labelled column groups; internal links from every `Source` cell to the main paragraph on the `Source text` sheet.\n\n## The sale, in plain language\n\nPetSmart's board started reviewing alternatives in mid-2014 after a weak May earnings release, stockholder letters, and public activism: JANA Partners filed a 9.9% Schedule 13D on July 3 and Longview publicly urged a sale and offered to roll over shares (July 7). The board's August 13 decision launched the sale process, announced August 19; an unnamed \"Industry Participant\" was deliberately kept out (antitrust, information-leak and delay concerns). Fifteen financial buyers signed confidentiality-and-standstill agreements in the first week of October, all did diligence, and six submitted non-binding indications by October 30. On November 3 the board advanced the four bidders at or above $80.00 — the Buyer Group, Bidder 2, and two unnamed finalists that then combined into \"Bidder 3\" (bidding units 4 → 3). On December 10 the Buyer Group bid $80.70 and Bidder 2 $80.35; Bidder 3 said its valuation would not exceed roughly the $78 market price and filed nothing. The ad hoc committee forced another round of bids; on December 12 the Buyer Group reached a best-and-final **$83.00 all cash** (after an oral $82.50), Bidder 2 confirmed $81.50 as its best and final, and Longview was cleared to join the Buyer Group with a share rollover. The board approved on December 13 and the merger agreement was signed and announced December 14, 2014, with Longview rolling over 3,012,050 shares (~$250M) and voting its 7,424,591 shares (~7.5%) for the deal. Observed outcome: **signing and announcement only** — completion was expected in 1H 2015 but is not observed in the filing; no go-shop and no competing proposal through the February 2, 2015 proxy date.\n\n## Most consequential review items\n\n- **Q1 – Round map.** One process; R1 anchored on the October NDA wave (rejected candidates R008/R009/R013 kept as rows), R2 at the 11/03 advancement, R3 at the November \"final bids\" request, with the December deadline events as revisions inside R3.\n- **Q2 – Deadlines.** 10/30 Enforced; 12/05 superseded before arrival (no milestone row); 12/10 Extended; 12/12 Enforced.\n- **Q3/Q4 – Counts and inferred closures.** Six IOI submitters preferred over the Reasons' \"5 bidder groups\"; the 9 non-submitters (15 − 6) and 2 eliminees (6 − 4) are the only inferred count rows, with bases flagged; the count-0 identity row for the unnamed $80–$85 bidder.\n- **Q5 – Identity mapping.** The two combiners are recommended to be the two unnamed finalists (the $80–$85 bidder and the third ≥$80 bidder); the ledger keeps them anonymous and puts the hypothesis in Questions.\n\n## Could not verify / unsupported features\n\n- LibreOffice crashed in this environment, so I could not render the workbook in a spreadsheet application; formulas were evaluated manually with openpyxl and match the intended totals (NDA 15; contacts 27; R1 bid units 6; R3 finals 2).\n- Drop-downs, collapsed groups and internal hyperlinks were created and checked programmatically (including XML integrity), but not exercised by clicking in Excel; no recalculation engine was available, so formula results should recompute on first open.","time":{"start":1789825293219,"end":1789825299591}}}
